# Monte Carlo estimate of pi

## Result
The pilot (`pilot.py`, unmodified) drew n = 1,678,300,000 uniform points in the unit square in 540 s; 1,318,121,554 fell inside the quarter circle.

| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| Estimate of pi | **3.141564** (3.1415636) |
| Standard error | 4.01e-05 |
| 95% confidence interval | **[3.141485, 3.141642]** (half-width 7.86e-05) |
| True pi | 3.141593 |
| Error (estimate - pi) | -2.90e-05 (z = -0.72) |

The interval covers the true value of pi. A Wilson interval agrees with the Wald interval to within 4.1e-09 at the endpoints, so the choice of interval method does not matter at this n.

## Method
Hit-or-miss sampling: pi is estimated as 4p, where p is the fraction of points inside the quarter circle. The interval is the normal-approximation (Wald) interval, 4p +/- 1.96 * 4 * sqrt(p(1-p)/n). It describes this one seeded run (seed 20261019), which would miss pi in about 5% of such runs; here it does not. The sample count depends on the speed of the shared host. Numbers are from `results/estimate.json`, computed from `results/pilot.json` by `analysis/analyze.py`.

